Why This Is Asked
Assess the ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued semantics in a constrained CSP and to design a propagation/backtracking approach that handles unknowns.

Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∧/∨ use min/max under T>U>F; A→B := ¬A ∨ B.
- Constraint propagation in a small CSP with unknowns; existence of a model containing U.
- Backtracking strategies to prune non-viable branches.

Code Example
```javascript
const V = {T:'T', F:'F', U:'U'}
functionNot(x){ if (x==='T') return 'F'; if (x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ if (a==='T' || b==='T') return 'T'; if (a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ if (a==='F' || b==='F') return 'F'; if (a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ return kleeneOr(functionNot(a), b) }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests time-expanded 3-valued reasoning and propagation strategies under Kleene semantics. It also checks ability to craft a concrete witness and reason about propagation order across time.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic; time-expanded CSP; forward propagation; A→B = ¬A ∨ B; monotone reasoning; time-dimension constraints; backtracking with preference for U.

## Code Example
```javascript
function imply(a,b){
  const notA = a==='T'?'F': a==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[notA] > order[b] ? notA : b;
}
```

